explanation:
This example loads practice data without using the internet.
```javascript
let state = { status: "idle" };
async function load(getData) {
  state = { status: "loading" };
  try {
    const data = await getData();
    state = { status: "success", data };
  } catch (error) {
    state = { status: "error", message: error.message };
  }
}
async function demo() {
  await load(() => Promise.resolve({ title: "Moon Notes" }));
  console.log(state.status, state.data.title);
}
await demo();
```
Output:
```text
success Moon Notes
```
`load` sets the state to loading, waits for the data, then saves success. `demo` waits before reading the final state. The app's example runner allows this outer `await`.

This version follows the failure path.
```javascript
let state = { status: "idle" };
async function load(getData) {
  state = { status: "loading" };
  try {
    const data = await getData();
    state = { status: "success", data };
  } catch (error) {
    state = { status: "error", message: error.message };
  }
}
async function demo() {
  await load(() => Promise.reject(new Error("Try again later")));
  console.log(state.status, state.message);
