explanation:
This example searches from P to S.
```javascript
const graph = { P: ["Q", "R"], Q: ["S"], R: ["S"], S: [] };
function search(start, target) {
  const queue = [[start, [start]]];
  const seen = new Set([start]);
  let visited = 0;
  while (queue.length) {
    const [node, path] = queue.shift();
    visited++;
    if (node === target) {
      return { path, visited };
    }
    for (const next of graph[node] || []) {
      if (!seen.has(next)) {
        seen.add(next);
        queue.push([next, [...path, next]]);
      }
    }
  }
  return { path: null, visited };
}
const result = search("P", "S");
console.log(result.path.join("->"), result.visited);
```
Output:
```text
P->Q->S 4
```
The nodes are checked in the order P, Q, R, S. S was first found through Q, so that is the path returned.

Depth-first search, or DFS, follows one branch before trying another. It uses a stack: the newest waiting entry goes next. `pop` removes that entry.
```javascript
const graph = { P: ["Q", "R"], Q: ["S"], R: [], S: ["P"] };
function depthVisits(start) {
  const stack = [start];
  const seen = new Set();
  const order = [];
  while (stack.length) {
    const node = stack.pop();
    if (seen.has(node)) {
      continue;
    }
    seen.add(node);
    order.push(node);
    for (const next of [...(graph[node] || [])].reverse()) {
      stack.push(next);
    }
  }
  return order;
}
console.log(depthVisits("P").join(","));
```
Output:
```text
P,Q,S,R
```
`continue` skips a node already seen. Reversing the copied neighbors makes the first listed one come off the stack first. DFS does not generally find the shortest route.

