This example receives the newest answer first.
```javascript
let latestId = 0;
const cancelledIds = new Set();
let visibleResult = "";
function beginSearch() {
  if (latestId > 0) {
    cancelledIds.add(latestId);
  }
  latestId++;
  return latestId;
}
function receiveResult(id, text) {
  if (id === latestId && !cancelledIds.has(id)) {
    visibleResult = text;
  }
}
const first = beginSearch();
const second = beginSearch();
receiveResult(second, "mountain answer");
receiveResult(first, "river answer");
console.log(visibleResult);
```
Output:
```text
mountain answer
```
Starting the second search marks the first as canceled. When the old answer arrives later, the guard ignores it.

The old answer must also be ignored if it arrives before the new answer.
